\[
\rho_{i}(\mathbf{R})=\frac{Q_{i}}{\alpha_{i}^{3} \pi^{3 / 2}} \exp \left(-\frac{\left|\mathbf{R}-\mathbf{R}_{i}\right|^{2}}{\alpha_{i}^{2}}\right)
\]

With this choice, the total energy of eq 3 can be calculated analytically
\[
\begin{aligned}
& E_{\text {tot }}\left(\left\{Q_{i}\right\},\left\{\mathbf{R}_{i}\right\}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text {atom }}}\left(E_{i}^{0}+\chi_{i} Q_{i}+\frac{1}{2}\left(J_{i i}+\frac{2 \gamma_{i i}}{\sqrt{\pi}}\right) Q_{i}^{2}\right) \\
& \quad+\sum_{i>j}^{N_{\text {atom }}} Q_{i} Q_{j} \frac{\operatorname{erf}\left(\gamma_{i j} R_{i j}\right)}{R_{i j}}
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\gamma_{\mathrm{ij}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}{ }^{2}+\alpha_{j}{ }^{2}}}\) and \(R_{i j}\) is the distance between atoms \(i\) and \(j\).
Since the energy is a quadratic function of atomic charges \(Q_{i}\), its minimization with respect to \(Q_{i}\) leads to a linear system of equations. The solution provides the equilibrated charges and \(E_{\text {tot }}\). Even though CENT depends only on local atomic electronegativities optimized in the training, a global redistribution of charges over the entire system is obtained.

The minimization of the energy expression in eq 3 has to be done under the linear constraint of conservation of the global charge. Varying this global charge allows us to treat not only neutral but also ionized systems. The constraint gives rise to a system of equations with an additional row and column. \({ }^{2}\) It can be written as
