\section*{Biographies}

Tsz Wai Ko received his bachelor degree in engineering physics in 2016 and his master of philosophy in theoretical and computational physics in 2018 from Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Currently he is a Ph.D. student in theoretical chemistry at the Georg-August-Universität Göttingen under the supervision of Prof. Jörg Behler. He is working on the development of machine learning potentials with accurate longrange interactions.
Jonas A. Finkler obtained his master's degree in physics from the University of Basel, where he joined the group of Stefan Goedecker as a Ph.D. student in 2019. His main research interests are the development of new algorithms and simulation methods ranging from advanced sampling techniques to machine learning potentials.
Stefan Goedecker obtained his diploma in physics in 1987 from the Technical University of Munich and his Ph.D. in 1992 from EPFL Lausanne. He was then a postdoc at the Cornell Theory Center and at the Max-Planck Institut for solid-state research in Stuttgart. In 2000, he obtained a staff position at CEA Grenoble. Since 2003, he has been a professor of computational physics at the University of Basel. He is interested in the development and improvement of algorithms for a wide spectrum of methods for atomistic simulations.
Jörg Behler graduated in chemistry at the Universität Dortmund in 2000. He then joined the Fritz-Haber-Institut der Max-PlanckGesellschaft in Berlin, where he obtained his Ph.D. in 2004. After a postdoctoral stay at the same place, he moved to the ETH Zurich in 2006. In 2007, he established an independent research group at the Ruhr-Universität Bochum. In 2017, he became a full professor for theoretical chemistry at the Georg-August-Universität Göttingen. His main research interest is the development of machine learning potentials with applications in chemistry and materials science.

